+/* 2026-07-31 black-background fix, dialog case (user report, live
+ * screenshot: the "Save configuration?" confirm dialog rendered solid
+ * black). Root cause, same family as .main_overlay/.main_grid/.heatergraph
+ * above: every Gtk.Dialog built by KlippyGtk.Dialog() (Save Config confirm,
+ * PID Calibrate confirm, every _confirm_send_action popup) is a fresh
+ * top-level GdkWindow realized at *runtime*, unlike the one persistent
+ * KlipperScreen window mapped at startup that the original diagnosis above
+ * confirmed does resolve @bg correctly. On this compositor-less raw
+ * X11/DRM setup, that runtime-realized surface never paints its
+ * CSS-declared background no matter what selector targets the window node
+ * itself -- confirmed by pixel-sampling four separate live screenshots:
+ * neither `window {background-color: @bg}` above nor `.dialog`'s own
+ * `background-color: @text-inv` below ever painted anything on the actual
+ * GtkWindow, class name or not.
+ *
+ * The fix that actually worked: paint content_area (the Gtk.Box that
+ * holds the dialog's real content, see KlippyGtk.py's Dialog(), class
+ * app_dialog_content, background-color: @bg in the shared rule above) --
+ * same idiom as main_grid inside screen.py's overlay, sidestepping the
+ * broken window-level paint entirely rather than trying to fix it. That
+ * alone left a black ring around the card exactly the size of the layout
+ * gap between content_area and the window edge (GTK margins/padding are
+ * empty space OUTSIDE a widget's own painted rectangle -- they're the
+ * PARENT's territory to paint, and this parent doesn't). So `margin: 1em`
+ * here and content_area's three set_margin_*(10/5/5/0) calls in
+ * KlippyGtk.py are both gone (zeroed), and this rule's `padding` moved to
+ * `.app_dialog_content` below instead, where it's applied to the widget
+ * that's actually proven to paint -- content_area now spans the entire
+ * window with no gap left for the broken layer to show through, and the
+ * equivalent inset for text/buttons comes from padding INSIDE that already
+ * -painted rectangle instead of margin outside it. */
 .dialog {
-    border: .1em solid @text-inv;
-    padding: 0.25em 2.5em;
     background-color: @text-inv;
-    margin: 1em;
+    margin: 0;
+    padding: 0;
+    /* border removed (was `.1em solid @text-inv`, 2026-07-31): it reserved
+     * ~1-2px of box-model space between content_area and the window's true
+     * edge, same class of gap as the margin/padding above -- confirmed live,
+     * a hairline strip of unpainted black remained at the bottom/right edge
+     * after zeroing margin+padding, exactly border-width sized. This border
+     * was never visible anyway once background-color started actually
+     * painting (it's the same @text-inv as the fill, zero contrast against
+     * it by design), so removing it costs nothing visually and closes the
+     * last of the gap. */
+}
+
+.app_dialog_content {
+    padding: 0.25em 2.5em;
 }
 
